  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increasing shower and thunderstorm chances along\nwith high humidity Sunday into much of next week.\n\nSfc high pressure remains in control tonight. Lows tonight will\nfall into the 60s to low 70s, with upper 50s along the \nAllegheny Front. Clouds begin to increase late in the night, but\nconditions are to remain dry.\n\nThe primary feature of interest for the weekend into early next\nweek is a slow moving upper low currently spinning near southern\nMinnesota into Iowa. Aside from a few isolated showers across\nhigher elevations of the Potomac Highlands, Saturday will be\nanother dry day for the Mid-Atlantic region. The most notable\nchange will be the shift to southerly flow which slowly\nincreases humidity levels. This becomes even more noticeable by\nSunday into the first full week of August. Additionally, expect\nanother uptick in temperatures with highs reaching the upper 80s\nto low 90s. Afternoon wind gusts push into the 20 to 25 mph\nrange depending on how mixed the boundary layer becomes. A\nmilder night lies ahead given mostly cloudy skies and warm\nadvection flow. Many spots should see lows in the upper 60s to\nlow 70s, with spotty mid 70s across the urban hubs.\n\nSunday and Monday present a more active pattern as the upstream\nclosed low slowly moves across the Ohio Valley. There is some\npotential for severe thunderstorms across areas west of I-95.\nAreas to the east largely stay under residual ridging aloft\nwhich should limit the convective potential. The Storm\nPrediction Center currently places the former region in a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5). Precipitable water values rise \ninto the 2 to 2.25 inch range which will enhance the potential \nfor heavy rainfall in any thunderstorm cores. The overall setup \nchanges little into Monday, albeit with the upper low in closer \nproximity to the Appalachians. A modified tropical air mass \nremains in place which comes with a weakly defined west-east \noriented frontal zone. Current limitations from a discernible \nflood risk would include (1) Weak low-level convergence, (2) \nLimited instability, and (3) Ongoing drought conditions. Will \ncontinue to monitor as additional model guidance arrives. \nOverall temperatures remain near to slightly below average owing\nto showers and cloud cover.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances continue each afternoon and evening \nbeyond Monday as we enter more of a typical Summertime pattern. The \nmean upper level trough and cutoff low will shift north and east \nwith a residual boundary leftover near the coast Tuesday into \nWednesday. As the low pulls further away several weak pieces of \nshortwave energy will pivot around it adding extra lift for \ndally shower and thunderstorm development. The greatest \nconcentration of storms will likely be over the terrain and \nin/around where the bay/river breeze sets up. Overall coverage will \nremain scattered, but likely amplified during the peak heating \nperiod given the high PWATS (1.5-2\"+) sitting over the region. With \nthat said, any flood threat would remain relegated to poor drainage \nareas pending repetitive rounds of storms or if storm track/sit over \nan urban location. Highs get back close to 90 Tuesday through Friday \nnext week. Overnight lows will fall back into the mid to upper\n60s.",
